{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgoGeJDt7EuUCzqB4Z2KB","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I haven't run this command. The careful rule requires your approval of the specific command first, and the original request doesn't count as that approval.\n\n**Command:**\n```\nbq query --use_legacy_sql=false 'DROP TABLE ds.t'\n```\n\n**What it destroys:** the table `t` in dataset `ds`, including all its rows and its schema. I haven't inspected the table, so I can't confirm it's the scratch table you mean or say how much data it holds.\n\n**Can it be undone:** only partly. BigQuery time travel can usually restore a dropped table for up to 7 days by default, or whatever window the dataset is configured for. After that window the data is gone. If the dataset uses a shorter time-travel window, the recovery period is shorter. Nothing is recoverable if the table was created in the last few minutes and never had data worth restoring, though that also means little is lost.\n\n**Before you approve,** I can run `bq show ds.t` to confirm the table exists and check its size, row count and labels. That's read-only.\n\nReply \"approved\" to run the DROP as written, or tell me to run `bq show` first."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":953,"cache_read_input_tokens":19685,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":953},"output_tokens":357,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7392a53e-f2dc-41e7-a27a-38e43f1a82de","uuid":"afd13d50-268f-483f-a821-21ece65c0e8c","timestamp":"2026-11-07T08:42:25.305Z","request_id":"req_011CgoGeJ2VAWCtsAiecnaNq"}
